{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-36216" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=3.$ Given that the point $P(2,3,9)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=6x-y$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-36216" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(2,3,9)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(2,3).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(2,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,3) & =6(2)-(3)=9\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-36216" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a9f77ab83ff0ba5aac801d22370afc5a675d44ad23b296279e6507db4f0f22b6"} {"question" "q-36216" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-36216" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-36216" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "9"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-71198" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P$ with $y$-coordinate $1$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=3{x}^{2}y-6x{y}^{2}$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-71198" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $y=1$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(2,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(2,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,1) & =3(2)^{2}(1)-6(2)(1)^{2} \\\\  & =12-12 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-71198" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a9f77ab83ff0ba5aac801d22370afc5a675d44ad23b296279e6507db4f0f22b6"} {"question" "q-71198" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-71198" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-71198" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-132584" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=-3.$ Given that the point $P(1,-3,8)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}+y\\sqrt{x}$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-132584" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=-3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(1,-3,8)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(1,-3).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(1,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,-3) & =\\frac{{1}^{2}+1}{{1}^{2}(-3)^{2}}+(-3)\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}-3(1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{25}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-132584" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a9f77ab83ff0ba5aac801d22370afc5a675d44ad23b296279e6507db4f0f22b6"} {"question" "q-132584" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-132584" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{16}{9}"] ["math" "-\\frac{25}{9}"] ["math" "-\\frac{7}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{15}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-132584" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{25}{9}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-64968" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P$ with $y$-coordinate $0$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\sqrt{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy+\\frac{\\pi }{4})$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-64968" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $y=0$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,0) & =\\sqrt{2}(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-64968" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a9f77ab83ff0ba5aac801d22370afc5a675d44ad23b296279e6507db4f0f22b6"} {"question" "q-64968" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-64968" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-64968" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-132579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P$ with $x$-coordinate $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+xy$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-132579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-1-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-132579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a9f77ab83ff0ba5aac801d22370afc5a675d44ad23b296279e6507db4f0f22b6"} {"question" "q-132579" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-132579" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1-\\pi "] ["math" "-\\pi "] ["math" "1+2\\pi "] ["math" "2-\\pi "] ["math" "\\pi -1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-132579" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1-\\pi "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-36301" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,0,8)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+4y+x$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-36301" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(-2,0,8)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,0)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(-2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,0)} & =(-2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+4(0)+(-2) \\\\  & =-2-2 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(-2,0)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,0)}(y-0) \\\\ z-8 & =-4(y-0) \\\\ z & =-4y+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,0,8)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=-4y+8\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-36301" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a9f77ab83ff0ba5aac801d22370afc5a675d44ad23b296279e6507db4f0f22b6"} {"question" "q-36301" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-36301" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=-4x\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=-4y+8\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=4x+8\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=4y+8\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=4y-8\\end{aligned}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-36301" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=-4y+8\\end{aligned}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-36441" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=0.5.$ Given that the point $P(0.5,2,4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=y+2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+\\frac{1}{x}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-36441" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=0.5.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(0.5,2,4)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(0.5,2)}.$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(0.5,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(0.5,2)} & =2+2(2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡((0.5)(2))+\\frac{1}{0.5} \\\\  & =2+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(0.5,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(0.5,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-4 & =4(y-2) \\\\ z & =4y-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0.5,2,4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=0.5 \\\\ z=4y-4\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-36441" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a9f77ab83ff0ba5aac801d22370afc5a675d44ad23b296279e6507db4f0f22b6"} {"question" "q-36441" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-36441" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=0.5 \\\\ z=-4y+12\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=0.5 \\\\ z=4x+2\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=0.5 \\\\ z=4y-12\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=0.5 \\\\ z=4y-4\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=4x+2\\end{aligned}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-36441" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\begin{aligned}x=0.5 \\\\ z=4y-4\\end{aligned}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-132590" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2,2\\pi )$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{xy}{\\pi }$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-132590" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2,2\\pi )$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-\\pi /2,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-\\pi /2,2)} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})(2)}{\\pi } \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(-\\pi /2,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-\\pi /2,2)}(x-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\ z-2\\pi  & =-2(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ z & =-2x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2,2\\pi )$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=-2x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-132590" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a9f77ab83ff0ba5aac801d22370afc5a675d44ad23b296279e6507db4f0f22b6"} {"question" "q-132590" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-132590" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=-2x+\\pi \\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=-2x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=2x-2\\pi \\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=x+2\\pi \\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=x-\\pi \\end{aligned}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-132590" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=-2x+\\pi \\end{aligned}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-132589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=e.$ Given that the point $P(e,2,3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-132589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=e.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(e,2,3)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(e,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(e,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(e,2) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(e,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(e,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-3 & =1(y-2) \\\\ z & =y+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(e,2,3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y+1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-132589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a9f77ab83ff0ba5aac801d22370afc5a675d44ad23b296279e6507db4f0f22b6"} {"question" "q-132589" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-132589" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=2y-1\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=2y-3\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y+1\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y+3\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y-2\\end{aligned}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-132589" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y+1\\end{aligned}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-71218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,2)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-71218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(0,1,2)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(0,1)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(0,1)} & =(0)^{2}+(1)^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1) \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(0,1)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(0,1)}(x-0) \\\\ z-2 & =1(x) \\\\ z & =x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0,1,2)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-71218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a9f77ab83ff0ba5aac801d22370afc5a675d44ad23b296279e6507db4f0f22b6"} {"question" "q-71218" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-71218" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=-2\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=-x+2\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=2\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=x+2\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=x-2\\end{aligned}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-71218" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=x+2\\end{aligned}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-36371" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=-3.$ Given that the point $P(2,-3,3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=6(x-1)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-36371" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=-3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(2,-3).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(2,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,-3) & =6(2-1)=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(2,-3) & ={f}_{x}(2,-3)(x-2) \\\\ z-3 & =6(x-2) \\\\ z-3 & =6x-12 \\\\ z & =6x-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,1,0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-3 \\\\ z=6x-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=-3,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=6x-9$ (or $6x-z-9=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨6,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨6,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-6\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-6⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-6⟩=⟨1,0,6⟩$ is also parallel to it." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-36371" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a9f77ab83ff0ba5aac801d22370afc5a675d44ad23b296279e6507db4f0f22b6"} {"question" "q-36371" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-36371" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-1,1,2⟩"] ["math" "⟨1,0,6⟩"] ["math" "⟨1,1,-2⟩"] ["math" "⟨2,0,-3⟩"] ["math" "⟨2,0,3⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-36371" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨1,0,6⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-132599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(2,1,4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={e}^{y-1}+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y-1)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-132599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(2,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,1) & ={e}^{1-1}+2(2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1-1) \\\\  & =1+4 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(2,1) & ={f}_{x}(2,1)(x-2) \\\\ z-4 & =5(x-2) \\\\ z & =5x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,1,4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=5x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=5x-6$ (or $5x-z-6=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨5,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨5,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-5\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-5⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-5⟩=⟨1,0,5⟩$ is also parallel to it." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-132599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a9f77ab83ff0ba5aac801d22370afc5a675d44ad23b296279e6507db4f0f22b6"} {"question" "q-132599" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-132599" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨1,0,2⟩"] ["math" "⟨1,0,5⟩"] ["math" "⟨2,0,3⟩"] ["math" "⟨3,0,1⟩"] ["math" "⟨3,0,5⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-132599" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨1,0,5⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-71220" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,-6,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\frac{y-x}{\\sqrt{x(y+x)}}$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-71220" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-2,-6).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-2,-6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-2,-6) & =\\frac{-6-(-2)}{\\sqrt{(-2)(-6+(-2))}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{4} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,-6) & ={f}_{y}(-2,-6)(y-(-6)) \\\\ z-1 & =-1(y+6) \\\\ z-1 & =-y-6 \\\\ z & =-y-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,-6,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=-y-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-y-5$ (or $y+z+5=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,-1,1⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-71220" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a9f77ab83ff0ba5aac801d22370afc5a675d44ad23b296279e6507db4f0f22b6"} {"question" "q-71220" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-71220" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨0,-1,1⟩"] ["math" "⟨0,-2,1⟩"] ["math" "⟨0,-6,1⟩"] ["math" "⟨0,1,1⟩"] ["math" "⟨0,6,1⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-71220" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨0,-1,1⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-71221" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(-1,1,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=3{e}^{x+y}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|xy|$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-71221" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-1,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-1,1) & =3{e}^{-1+1}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(-1)\\cdot 1| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =3-0 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-1,1) & ={f}_{x}(-1,1)(x-(-1)) \\\\ z-(-1) & =3(x+1) \\\\ z+1 & =3x+3 \\\\ z & =3x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-1,1,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=3x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=3x+2$ (or $3x-z+2=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨3,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-3\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-3⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-3⟩=⟨1,0,3⟩$ is also parallel to it." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-71221" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a9f77ab83ff0ba5aac801d22370afc5a675d44ad23b296279e6507db4f0f22b6"} {"question" "q-71221" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-71221" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-1,0,3⟩"] ["math" "⟨1,0,-3⟩"] ["math" "⟨1,0,1⟩"] ["math" "⟨1,0,3⟩"] ["math" "⟨3,0,1⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-71221" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨1,0,3⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-64971" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,1,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=2(x+y)-2$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-64971" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(1,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =2(1+1)-2=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,1) & ={f}_{x}(1,1)(x-1) \\\\ z-1 & =2(x-1) \\\\ z-1 & =2x-2 \\\\ z & =2x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,1,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=2x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=2x-1$ (or $2x-z-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨2,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $-i-2\\,k$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(-i-2\\,k)=i+2\\,k$ is also parallel to it." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-64971" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a9f77ab83ff0ba5aac801d22370afc5a675d44ad23b296279e6507db4f0f22b6"} {"question" "q-64971" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-64971" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3i+\\,k"] ["math" "2i-2\\,k"] ["math" "i+2\\,k"] ["math" "i+\\,k"] ["math" "i-2\\,k"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "q-64971" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "i+2\\,k"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ec12a6edf8506dcb8fced4e6484124e7912f5c4f003350240109667f5f96a36"} {"question" "e-3068" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P(2,1,8)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_y = \\dfrac{4x}y - 4x^2y,$ find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/example-3068.json" "file_sha256" "8fc978663802cb7e3afc41ff4bb45bc54323094b5ac126ee020afbaead1b43b7"} {"question" "e-3068" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ So, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(2,1,8)$ will be given by the partial derivative $f_y(2,1).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $f_y(x,y)$ at $(2,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,1) & =\\frac{4(2)}{(1)}-4(2)^{2}(1) \\\\  & =8-16 \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/example-3068.json" "file_sha256" "8fc978663802cb7e3afc41ff4bb45bc54323094b5ac126ee020afbaead1b43b7"} {"question" "e-3069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=\\pi.$ Given that the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_x = y\\cos(xy) + \\cos y,$ find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/example-3069.json" "file_sha256" "e97df7b9b702a5068364a27cbf9c189a081af987cc150694c0e111bed37deeb6"} {"question" "e-3069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z = f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=\\pi.$ So, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\dfrac {\\partial z} {\\partial x} \\Bigg|_{(1,\\pi)}.$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\dfrac {\\partial z} {\\partial x}$ at $(1,\\pi)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\pi )} & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot \\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =\\pi (-1)+(-1) \\\\  & =-\\pi -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,\\pi )} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\pi )}(x-1) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(-\\pi -1)(x-1) \\\\ z+1 & =(-\\pi -1)x+\\pi +1 \\\\ z & =-(\\pi +1)x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above represents a line in the $xz$-plane. The $xz$-plane through the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ has the equation $y=\\pi.$\n\nSo, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ is given by the intersection of the following planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\pi  \\\\ z=-(\\pi +1)x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/example-3069.json" "file_sha256" "e97df7b9b702a5068364a27cbf9c189a081af987cc150694c0e111bed37deeb6"} {"question" "e-3070" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,1)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_x =  \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2} \\pi}{4} \\sin{\\left(2 x + \\dfrac{\\pi y}{4} \\right)},$ find a tangent vector to $C$ at $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/example-3070.json" "file_sha256" "295e190d64d9a404b899c98852eb5ca64899f183fb71ef6ec9c88429f31bfdb5"} {"question" "e-3070" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ So, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative $f_x(0,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $f_x(x,y)$ at $(0,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(0)+\\frac{\\pi (1)}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(0,1) & ={f}_{x}(0,1)(x-0) \\\\ z-1 & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}x \\\\ z & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above represents a line in the $xz$-plane. The $xz$-plane through the point $P(0,1,1)$ has the equation $y=1.$\n\nSo, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0,1,1)$ is given by the intersection of the following planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=\\frac{\\pi }{4}x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find a tangent vector, we can find a normal vector to each plane and compute their cross product.\n\n- For the plane $y = 1,$ we write $0x + 1y + 0z = 1$ and find the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 0,1,0 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $z = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} x +1,$ we write $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} x + 0y + 1z = 1$ and find the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\left\\langle -\\dfrac{\\pi}4,0,1 \\right\\rangle.$\n\nComputing the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨-\\frac{\\pi }{4},0,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\pi /4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we've found a tangent vector, and this could be our final answer. However, just to make our answer a little nicer, we can multiply the vector by $4$ to yield a simpler, parallel vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot (i+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,k)=4\\,i+\\pi \\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/example-3070.json" "file_sha256" "295e190d64d9a404b899c98852eb5ca64899f183fb71ef6ec9c88429f31bfdb5"} {"question" "q-36371" "field" "selection" "attribute" "an